WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.040
Thanks for joining me on fascinating frontiers, episode 132.

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:06.800
It's July 15th, 2026.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.740
Some fascinating developments to cover today.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:13.960
China's Tianwen 2 probe has reached a near-earth asteroid

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:17.640
after a billion kilometer chase, raising the possibility

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.620
that the target is a long-lost lunar fragment.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.020
The spacecraft has closed within 20 kilometers

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:25.460
of its target asteroid.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.860
It is now collecting spectra and rotation data

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:29.780
during the approach.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:33.180
The primary goal is to test whether the asteroid

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:35.420
represents captured lunar ejecta.

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:38.600
These observations mark the first close-range

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.720
compositional measurements of a potential moon fragment.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:45.280
Follow-up measurements will focus on surface composition

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:46.840
and rotation characteristics.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.520
Next data releases are expected once initial spectra

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.500
and imaging sequences are processed.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.600
The encounter supplies the first direct compositional measurements

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:59.600
that could distinguish between captured lunar material

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.620
and native asteroid origins.

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:05.080
The Tianwen 2 data will test whether the body

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:07.220
originated as lunar ejecta.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:11.060
Confirmation would reshape models of how material moves

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:14.200
between the Earth's moon system and near-earth orbits.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.140
Now shifting to a high-energy detection

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:18.720
in a very different regime.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:23.300
The very large array recorded an off-axis radio after glow

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:27.400
after an intermediate mass black hole disrupted a passing star.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:31.300
The event provides rare evidence that such black holes

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:32.800
can launch powerful jets.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:35.940
Observations can strain the mass range

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:39.280
and accretion behavior of this elusive class of objects.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:43.220
Additional radio monitoring will track how the jet evolves

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:44.480
over coming months.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.620
Radio observations captured the afterglow

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:50.540
at multiple frequencies, revealing how the outflow

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:53.220
interacts with the interstellar medium over time.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.680
We still do not know what fraction of intermediate mass black holes

00:01:57.680 --> 00:01:59.260
can generate such outflows

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:01.600
or why some disruptions produce jets

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:03.700
while others remain radio quiet.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.520
If the sun were compressed into a sphere

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.020
the size of a large city

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:12.500
an intermediate mass black hole would still be 10 times more massive

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:16.180
yet occupy a volume no larger than a modest mountain range.

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:19.220
When such an object passes near a star

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:22.340
tidal forces stretch the star into a thin stream

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:25.640
that spirals inward, releasing enormous energy

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:28.080
as the material crosses the event horizon.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:29.900
In the recent detection

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:33.300
that inflow apparently powered a relativistic jet

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.720
pointed slightly away from our line of sight,

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:38.560
producing the observed radio afterglow.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.860
The jet itself travels at nearly the speed of light

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:43.900
and can remain detectable for months

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:45.960
as it expands into surrounding gas.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:51.340
On a different note, orbital imaging has revealed striking surface features on Mars.

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:56.420
The Mars Express Orbiter captured a wide field of wind sculpted dunes

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:59.380
that appear metallic in tone inside Kaiser Crater.

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:02.400
These features formed over billions of years

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.260
under persistent Martian winds.

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:08.240
The image reveals fine-scale ripples across the dune field

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:10.460
that trace past atmospheric patterns.

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:13.000
Scientists will compare the new view

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:16.600
with earlier orbital passes to track any seasonal changes.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:19.360
The observation adds another data point

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:22.680
to ongoing robotic surveys of Mars surface evolution.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.420
The wide field view highlights how wind action

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:29.860
has shaped the crater interior across multiple epochs.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:33.400
Crew members performed the first medical x-ray imaging

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:34.540
during a space flight.

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:38.660
The procedure demonstrates that routine diagnostic capability

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:41.880
can now be maintained without returning samples to Earth.

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:45.420
Equipment was adapted to operate in microgravity

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:47.700
while meeting radiation safety standards.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.780
Future expeditions may expand the technique

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:53.980
to monitor bone density and other crew health metrics.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:57.420
This milestone supports sustained human presence

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:00.000
by enabling in-c2 medical assessment.

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:04.380
The imaging run confirmed that standard radiographic hardware

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:07.460
can function reliably inside a spacecraft cabin.

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:10.460
NASA has selected 41 technologies

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:12.440
for future moon and Mars missions.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.580
The projects cover propulsion, life support,

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.440
resource extraction, and navigation systems.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:21.560
Each technology targets specific challenges

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:24.600
for sustained operations beyond low Earth orbit.

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:26.940
Teams will advance the concepts

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:29.400
through ground testing and prototype development.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.620
Progress reports will feed into broader planning

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:35.420
for crude lunar and Martian surface activities.

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:38.940
The selections directly support the Artemis architecture

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:41.400
and future robotic precursor missions.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:44.920
The funded efforts span multiple technical domains

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:47.000
needed for extended surface stays.

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:49.540
The curiosity rover has moved

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:51.440
through several distinct rock layers

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:53.560
mapped from orbit in gale crater.

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:56.020
Each unit shows different textures

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:57.180
and mineral signatures

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:00.000
that record changing environmental conditions.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:03.620
Atmospheric measurements continue alongside

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:04.960
the geological work.

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:07.220
The campaign will help reconstruct

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:09.780
how the crater floor evolved over time.

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:12.880
This phase of the mission builds on prior

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:16.020
traverses by sampling a new sequence of mapped units.

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:18.920
The rover is now examining contacts

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:21.200
between previously identified strata

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:24.300
to understand transitions in depositional environments.

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:27.300
A new paper proposes new signatures

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:30.740
as an intermediate category in astrobiology searches.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:33.600
The study argues that current searches

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:36.180
focus too narrowly on either simple life

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:38.020
or technological civilizations.

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:41.060
It proposes looking for intermediate signs

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:42.700
of intelligence that could appear

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:45.600
after billions of years of biological evolution.

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:48.880
The concept fills a gap between microbial

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:50.940
biosignatures and radio signals.

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:54.500
Follow-up work will identify observable markers

00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:56.480
that future telescopes could target.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:05:59.500
The approach opens a new search category

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:01.480
between established biosignature

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:03.200
and techno-signature frameworks.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:06.800
The author's outline several potential observables

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:09.560
that could be pursued with next-generation instruments.

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:13.700
Measurements of more than 150,000 ancient stars

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:16.320
show the oldest formed at the epoch

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:17.900
predicted by the standard model.

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:19.720
The result adds weight

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:21.420
against certain proposed solutions

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:22.580
to the Hubble tension.

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:25.000
Precise age determinations

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:27.100
came from combining spectroscopic

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:28.440
and photometric surveys.

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:30.900
Continued surveys will refine

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:34.140
the age distribution across different galactic regions.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:36.820
The data set strengthens the standard

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:38.200
cosmological timeline

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:40.880
within ongoing Hubble tension investigations.

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:44.600
The large sample size allows statistical checks

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:46.140
on formation epochs

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:47.820
across varying metallocities

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:49.280
and galactic positions.

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:52.000
Researchers at Durham University

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:54.160
identified a dense stellar nursery

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:55.500
at the heart of a galaxy

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:57.540
more than 9 billion years old.

00:06:58.220 --> 00:07:00.000
The structure offers a direct view

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.180
of early galaxy assembly processes.

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:05.700
High-resolution data reveal rapid star formation

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:07.360
within a compact region.

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:09.640
Further observations will test

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:11.200
whether similar cores appear

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:12.740
in even older systems.

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:15.140
The finding extends the timeline

00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:17.060
for observing the earliest phases

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:18.760
of galactic core development.

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:20.880
The core appears embedded

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:22.620
in a larger galactic structure

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:24.660
already undergoing active assembly

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:25.740
at that epoch.

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:29.100
New measurements quantify the loose surface layer

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:30.240
that covers the moon.

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:32.500
Thickness varies with location

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:34.780
and influences landing site selection

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:36.640
and resource extraction planning.

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:39.100
The study combines orbital radar

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:40.780
and surface sampling data.

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:42.920
Future landers will test the maps

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:45.360
during site characterization activities.

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:48.080
These maps provide practical inputs

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:50.160
for Artemis landing site evaluations

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:52.140
and resource prospecting.

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:54.320
The derived thickness values

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:55.920
differ systematically between

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:57.420
Highlands and Mayor regions.

00:07:58.140 --> 00:07:59.880
Four white dwarfs were identified

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:01.860
through their gravitational influence

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:03.320
rather than direct light.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:05.360
Each object is denser

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:06.840
than typical white dwarfs

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:09.280
yet remains invisible in standard surveys.

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:12.380
The discovery expands the known population

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:14.140
of nearby compact remnants.

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:17.080
Additional radial velocity monitoring

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:19.000
will determine their exact masses

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:20.120
and cooling ages.

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:22.520
The detections increase the census

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:24.060
of nearby white dwarfs

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:26.000
available for detailed follow-up.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:28.240
The companions are red dwarfs

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:29.360
whose orbital motions

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:31.340
revealed the hidden dense objects.

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:33.140
A new mission concept

00:08:33.140 --> 00:08:35.480
called Skyfall would send three aircraft

00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:36.800
to survey regions

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:38.800
inaccessible to rovers on Mars.

00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:41.640
The aircraft would map climate history

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:43.260
and identify landing sites

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:44.780
for future human missions.

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:46.940
The design builds on lessons

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:48.500
from the ingenuity helicopter.

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:51.160
Concept studies will assess power

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:52.740
and communication requirements

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:53.940
for extended flights.

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:57.440
Skyfall would extend the robotic exploration program

00:08:57.440 --> 00:08:59.860
by adding aerial reconnaissance capabilities

00:08:59.860 --> 00:09:01.420
to Mars surface studies.

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:03.360
The aircraft are envisioned

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:05.000
to operate at altitudes

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:06.940
that permit broad regional coverage

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:08.100
in a single flight.

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:11.400
The Artemis II crew met the European teams

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:13.960
responsible for the Orion Service Module

00:09:13.960 --> 00:09:15.460
at ESTEC.

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:18.200
The visit covered integration status

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:19.840
and operational procedures.

00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:21.500
Data from the meeting

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:22.920
will inform final checks

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:23.720
before the mission.

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:25.620
The service module

00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:26.960
remains a critical element

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:28.580
for life support and propulsion

00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:29.560
during the flight.

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:32.700
This review advances the Artemis architecture

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:34.660
by confirming hardware readiness

00:09:34.660 --> 00:09:36.480
for the crude lunar trajectory.

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:39.160
The astronauts examined interfaces

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:40.480
that will supply power,

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:41.120
propulsion,

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:43.140
and thermal control for the flyby.

00:09:44.020 --> 00:09:45.820
NASA astronaut Anil Menon

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:48.320
and two Ross Cosmos cosmonauts

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:49.960
entered the space station laboratory

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:52.480
after docking for Expedition 74.

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:54.640
They will conduct experiments

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:56.180
during an eight-month stay.

00:09:56.980 --> 00:09:58.120
Daily updates will appear

00:09:58.120 --> 00:09:59.980
on the NASA Space Station blog.

00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:01.680
The crew is scheduled

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:03.900
to return in April 2027.

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:06.300
The arrival continues the cadence

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:07.360
of crew rotations

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:09.100
supporting ongoing commercial

00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:10.660
and international operations

00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:11.980
in low-Earth orbit.

00:10:12.620 --> 00:10:13.680
The new members join

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:14.660
an existing crew

00:10:14.660 --> 00:10:16.160
already performing a range

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:18.020
of science and maintenance tasks.

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:21.680
Objects 69,999

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:23.220
has been assigned

00:10:23.220 --> 00:10:25.240
to a previously uncatalogged

00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:26.700
piece of the 1958

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:28.200
Vanguard 1 satellite.

00:10:28.780 --> 00:10:30.260
The fragment now carries

00:10:30.260 --> 00:10:31.800
the international designation

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:34.940
1958-002D.

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:37.480
The assignment marks the last entry

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:40.160
before the catalog-numbering system rolls over.

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:41.920
Analysts will continue

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:43.400
to track the object's orbit

00:10:43.400 --> 00:10:45.080
with existing radar assets.

00:10:45.980 --> 00:10:47.100
The cataloging step

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:49.080
closes the pre-rollover sequence

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:50.340
for debris from one

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:51.860
of the earliest orbital missions.

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:53.660
The piece had remained

00:10:53.660 --> 00:10:55.260
unnumbered despite decades

00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:56.400
of orbital tracking.

00:10:57.080 --> 00:10:58.200
New orbital data

00:10:58.200 --> 00:10:59.380
and mission concepts

00:10:59.380 --> 00:11:00.520
continue to refine

00:11:00.520 --> 00:11:02.040
our picture of both nearby

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:03.320
and distant worlds.

